            {
              "id": "7e95b841-7c3e-37e2-939d-e619e046e0c1",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the vulnerability \"Avoid Using Weak or Non-Cryptographic Random Number Generators\" refers to the use of weak or non-cryptographic random number generators to generate values that are intended to be unpredictable. These values could be used in security-sensitive contexts, such as generating random passwords, cryptographic keys, or other similar security parameters. \n\nWeak or non-cryptographic random number generators do not provide a sufficient level of unpredictability because they generate numbers that can be reasonably predicted by an attacker. This could lead to a variety of security issues, such as password cracking, session hijacking, and other similar attacks.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use a strong, cryptographic random number generator that is designed for security-sensitive contexts. In C++, this could be achieved by using the `std::random_device` class from the `<random>` library, which is designed to generate truly random numbers.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a possible fix for the given code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\n// ...\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 g(rd());\nstd::shuffle(m_state.mt.begin(), m_state.mt.end(), g);\n```\n\nIn this code, `std::random_device` is used to seed the Mersenne Twister pseudo-random number generator (`std::mt19937`), which is then used to shuffle the `m_state.mt` vector.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<random>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
