              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the vulnerability \"Avoid Using Non-Cryptographic or Weak Random Number Generators\" refers to the use of random number generators that are not cryptographically secure. This can lead to predictable randomness which can be exploited by an attacker to predict future random numbers generated by the system. This can lead to a variety of security issues, such as predictable session identifiers, passwords, or cryptographic keys.\n\nThe specific vulnerability sink in the provided code is the use of the `shuffle` function with a non-cryptographic random number generator (`m_state.mt[i340_4]`).\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use a cryptographically secure random number generator. In C++, this can be achieved by using the `<random>` library and specifically the `std::random_device` class, which is designed to produce a non-deterministic random number.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `shuffle` function with a cryptographically secure random number generator. Here is an example of how to do this:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 g(rd());\n\nstd::shuffle(v340.begin(), v340.end(), g);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<random>` library to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
